f \\(T_3\\) are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(y=1\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Any two of these three points are collinear on a line that is either horizontal (\\((1,2)-(2,2)\\)), vertical (\\((1,2)-(1,3)\\)), or slope \\(-1\\) (\\((2,2)-(1,3)\\)).  \n  Hence none of the remaining pairs can be placed on a sunny line.  \n  Two sunny lines cannot cover the three remaining points \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(y=2\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,3)\\).  \n  The only sunny line that can contain two of the remaining points is the line through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  y-3=-2(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;y=-2x+5,\n  \\]\n  which indeed contains \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n  The two points still uncovered are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); any line through them is horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n  Thus the two sunny lines cannot cover all four remaining points \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(y=3\\).**  \n  Covered point: \\((1,3)\\).  \n  Remaining points: five points.  \n  A sunny line can contain at most two of these five (any three are never collinear with a sunny slope).  \n  Two sunny lines can therefore cover at most four points, leaving at least one uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\nHence a horizontal non\u2011sunny line cannot work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The non\u2011sunny line is vertical \\(\\;x=c\\)\n\nPossible values are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(x=1\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Any two of these three points lie on a forbidden line:  \n  \\((2,1)-(3,1)\\) is horizontal, \\((2,1)-(2,2)\\) is vertical, \\((3,1)-(2,2)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Thus no sunny line can contain two of them, and two sunny lines cannot cover all three \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(x=2\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(3,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  The only sunny line that can contain two of the remaining points is the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  y-2=-\\tfrac12(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\n  \\]\n  which indeed contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \n  The two points still uncovered are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\); any line through them is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n  Therefore the two sunny lines cannot cover all four remaining points \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(x=3\\).**  \n  Covered point: \\((3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: five points.  \n  As in the horizontal case, two sunny lines can cover at most four of them, leaving at least one uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus a vertical non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The non\u2011sunny line has slope \\(-1\\) \\(\\;(x+y=c)\\)\n\nPossible constants are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(x+y=2\\).**  \n  Covered point: \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Remaining five points cannot be covered by two sunny lines (same counting argument as above).  \n\n* **\\(x+y=3\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The only sunny line that can contain two of these is the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\):\n  \\[\n  y=x,\n  \\]\n  which is sunny.  \n  The two points still uncovered are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); the line through them has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n  Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover all four remaining points \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(x+y=4\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Any pair among these three points lies on a forbidden line (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n  Consequently two sunny lines cannot cover all three \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) also fails.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible choice for the single non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, **no** set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
