radiction.\n\nThus a vertical non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑sunny line has slope \\(-1\\) \\(\\;(x+y=c)\\)\n\nPossible constants are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(x+y=2\\).**  \n  Covered point: \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The five remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines (maximum four points can be covered, as shown in the previous cases). – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(x+y=3\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Among these, the only sunny line containing two points is the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\):\n  \\[\n  y=x,\n  \\]\n  which is sunny.  \n  The two points still uncovered are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); the line through them has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  \n  Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover all four remaining points – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(x+y=4\\).**  \n  Covered points: \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Any pair among these three lies on a forbidden line (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n  Consequently each of the three points would require its own sunny line, but only two sunny lines are available – contradiction.\n\nThus a non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) also fails.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every admissible choice for the single non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction, **no** set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
