a+b=n+2,\\qquad a,b\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nThese new points form the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}=\\{(a,n+2-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTake the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) and add the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **non\u2011sunny**.  \nNow delete the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) because a vertical line is a perfectly admissible non\u2011sunny line).  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) that were covered by the removed line \\(x=1\\) lie on the new diagonal \\(D\\) because the only points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(1\\) are \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\), and each of them satisfies \\(1+b\\le n+1\\); after the addition of the point \\((1,n+1)\\) (which belongs to \\(D\\)) the whole column \\(x=1\\) is now covered by \\(D\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) are untouched, and every point of the new diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) lies on \\(D\\) by definition.  \nHence every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nThe line \\(D\\) is non\u2011sunny and the removed line \\(x=1\\) was also non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays exactly \\(k\\).\n\nThus we have produced a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 Construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\)\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) just obtained.  \nRecall that the only point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(n+1\\) is \\((n+1,1)\\); this point is covered by the diagonal line \\(D:x+y=n+2\\).\n\nReplace the line \\(D\\) by any **sunny** line that passes through \\((n+1,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=-\\frac12x+\\frac{n+3}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) and therefore is sunny.  \nAll points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) other than \\((n+1,1)\\) remain covered because they were already covered by lines different from \\(D\\).  \nThe point \\((n+1,1)\\) is now covered by the new sunny line \\(S\\).\n\nConsequently the resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k+1\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completion of the Induction\n\nLemma\u202f1 provides the required families for \\(n=3\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that, assuming the statement true for a given \\(n\\), it is true for \\(n+1\\) for **all** admissible values of \\(k\\) (both \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\)).  \nHence, by induction on \\(n\\), for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe have only \\(n\\) lines in total; a line is either sunny or non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nObviously \\(k\\) cannot be negative.  \nThus no integer outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"

