n for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All points of \\(T_n\\)\n   lie on one of them, and none of the lines is sunny.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=i\\,x+\\bigl(n+1-i-i^{2}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n   One checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap T_n=\\{(i,\\,n+1-i),\\;(i+1,\\,n-i)\\},\n   \\qquad(1\\le i\\le n-1),\n   \\]\n   and \\(L_n\\) contains the single point \\((n,1)\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny\n   lines together cover all \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (there are \\(n-k-1\\) of them) and the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Both (1) and (2) are non‑sunny.  The line \\(D\\) meets each remaining\n   column \\(x=i\\;(n-k\\le i\\le n)\\) in exactly one point,\n   namely \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;y=j\\,x+\\bigl(n+1-j-j^{2}\\bigr).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   As in the all‑sunny construction, each \\(S_j\\) is sunny and\n\n   \\[\n   S_j\\cap T_n=\\{(j,\\,n+1-j),\\;(j+1,\\,n-j)\\},\n   \\qquad(1\\le j\\le k-1),\n   \\]\n   while \\(S_k\\) contains the single point \\((k,1)\\).\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n\n   *Column \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-k-1\\).*  \n   All points of this column lie on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) from (1).\n\n   *Column \\(x=i\\) with \\(n-k\\le i\\le n\\).*  \n   The point \\((i,\\,n+1-i)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n   The remaining points of the column have ordinates\n   \\(b\\le n-i\\).  For each such point we have\n   \\[\n   (i,b) = (i,\\,n+1-i) - (0,\\,n+1-2i-b)\n   \\]\n   and one checks that it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{i-