y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n  \\]\n  (Each of them passes through two points of \\(T_3\\); a direct substitution\n  shows that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on one of the three lines.)\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for **every** integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\nWe prove that the same statement holds for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Keeping the number of sunny lines\n\nFrom \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) construct a family for \\(T_{n+1}\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{\\,L_{\\text{new}}\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nL_{\\text{new}}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) is not sunny (its slope is \\(-1\\)).\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by the original \\(n\\) lines,\nand the points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\) are precisely those with\n\\(a+b=n+2\\), which lie on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\).\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) covers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly the\nsame \\(k\\) sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we obtain a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) with\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and consider the family\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\) constructed in 2.1.\nThe diagonal line \\(x+y=2\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\); it contains\nthe single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\nChoose any sunny line \\(S\\) that passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is not\nparallel to any line already present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\); for instance\n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x .\n\\]\n\nReplace the line \\(x+y=2\\) by \\(S\\) and denote the new family by\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)\\).\nSince \\(S\\) contains \\((1,1)\\), all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) remain covered.\nThe line \\(S\\) is sunny, while the removed line was not sunny; consequently\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k+1)\\) contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore, starting from a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines we can obtain a\ncovering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Completion of the induction\n\nFrom the base case \\(n=3\\) we have coverings for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\nApplying the operation of §2.1 we obtain coverings for the same values of\n\\(k\\) for every larger \\(n\\).\nApplying repeatedly the operation of §2.2 we can raise the number of sunny\nlines step by step, eventually reaching any integer up to the total number\nof lines, i.e. up to \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently, for **every** integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and **every** integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of other values\n\nBy definition we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny lines\ncannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence no integer \\(k\\) outside\nthe interval \\([0,n]\\) can be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and\n\\(n\\), inclusive:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\,}.\n\\]"
