urations with exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nKeep the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(L_c\\) for \\(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\).  \nThe diagonal \\(L_2\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).  \nReplace \\(L_2\\) by any sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\); for definiteness take  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) is sunny because its slope \\(2\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nIt contains the point \\((1,1)\\); all other points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered by the unchanged diagonals \\(L_c\\ (c\\ge 3)\\).  \nConsequently we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly one sunny line, proving that \\(k=1\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of two or more sunny lines (\\(k\\ge 2\\))\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nFor any integer \\(c\\ge 3\\) the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\), namely \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\).\n\n*Proof.* Both points satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=c\\le n+1\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\). \u220e\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a line contains two distinct points of the same diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(c\\ge 2\\)), then its slope is \\(-1\\); consequently the line is **not** sunny.\n\n*Proof.* Let the two points be \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a+b=a'+b'=c\\).  \nThe slope is \\(\\dfrac{b'-b}{a'-a}= \\dfrac{c-a'-c+a}{a'-a}= -1\\). \u220e\n\n#### Consequence of Lemma\u202f2  \nA sunny line can intersect a given diagonal in **at most one** point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) contains \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal D\\) be the set of all non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nEvery non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or parallel to a diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nIf a non\u2011sunny line is not a diagonal, then it meets each diagonal in at most one point; a diagonal line coincides with exactly one diagonal.\n\nConsider a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 it contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBecause of Lemma\u202f2, each sunny line of \\(\\mathcal C\\) can cover **at most one** of those points.  \nIf the diagonal itself is **absent** from \\(\\mathcal C\\), the remaining lines that can cover its points are:\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines (each at most one point), and\n* the non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \n\nEvery non\u2011sunny line that is not the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) meets this diagonal in at most one point, and any other diagonal in \\(\\mathcal D\\) also meets it in exactly one point (different from the points already covered by the sunny lines).  \nHence the total number of distinct points of the diagonal that can be covered by all lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;|\\mathcal D|\\;=\\;k\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\nBut the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) contains \\(c-1\\ge 2\\) points, and for \\(c\\ge 4\\) we have \\(c-1>n\\) only when \\(c>n+1\\), which never occurs.  \nMore importantly, the **set** of points of the diagonal is larger than the number of lines that intersect it **distinctly**: each line contributes at most one point, so to cover all \\(c-1\\) points we would need at least \\(c-1\\) distinct lines intersecting the diagonal.  \nSince \\(c-1\\ge 2\\) and we have at most \\(n\\) lines, this is not a contradiction yet.  \n\nThe crucial observation is that the only diagonal that can be omitted without leaving an uncovered point is \\(x+y=2\\), which contains a single point \\((1,1)\\).  \nIndeed, suppose a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 3\\) is missing from \\(\\mathcal C\\).  \nAll other diagonals are present (otherwise we would be missing more than one diagonal).  \nEach of those remaining diagonals meets the missing diagonal in exactly one point, and those intersection points are **different** for different diagonals because the system of equations  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx+y=c\\\\\nx+y=c' \n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad (c'\\neq c)\n\\]\n\nhas no solution.  \nConsequently the only points of the missing diagonal that can be covered by the other diagonals are the \\(n-1\\) intersection points, one per other diagonal.  \nSince the missing diagonal has at least two points, at least one of its points would remain uncovered unless a sunny line passes through it.  \nA sunny line can pass through at most one point of that diagonal (Lemma\u202f2), so after using all \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(k\\) points of the missing diagonal.  \nBecause the diagonal has at least two points and \\(k\\ge 2\\), we would need **all** its points to be covered by sunny lines, which would require the sunny lines to be collinear with the diagonal \u2013 impossible because sunny lines are not allowed to have slope \\(-1\\).  \nThus a diagonal with \\(c\\ge 3\\) cannot be omitted.\n\nTherefore the only diagonal that may be omitted is \\(x+y=2\\).  \nReplacing this diagonal by a sunny line yields exactly one sunny line (the construction of Section\u202f2).  \nIf we attempted to obtain two sunny lines, we would have to replace **another** diagonal, which we have just proved impossible.  \n\nHence any admissible configuration can contain at most one sunny line.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited configurations for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\), and we have proved that no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines.  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0\\text{ or }k=1\\,}.\n\\]"

