).  Any sunny line that already contains \\((1,2)\\) (resp. \\((2,1)\\)) would have to be vertical or horizontal, which is forbidden.  Hence \\((1,1)\\) cannot lie on either of the two sunny lines that cover the points of \\(D_3\\).  It must therefore be covered by a third line, which would have to be non\u2011sunny.  This contradicts the hypothesis that exactly two lines are sunny.  \u220e\n\n   Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible** for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Step\u202f5.1 \u2013 diagonal lines.*  \n   Keep the \\(n-k\\) largest diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   They are non\u2011sunny and together contain all points whose sum is at least \\(k+2\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5.2 \u2013 the omitted diagonals.*  \n   The omitted diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their total set of points is  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f5.3 \u2013 the \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y-(k+1-i)=\\;1\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (1) can be rewritten as  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x - i + (k+1-i)=x + (k+1-2i).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny (it is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   **Incidence with the omitted diagonals.**  \n   The point \\((i,\\,k+1-i)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\) and also on \\(L_i\\) (substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=k+1-i\\)).  Thus each \\(L_i\\) meets the *largest* omitted diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\) in a distinct point.  \n\n   For any smaller omitted diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le k\\) we have \\(c-1\\le k-1\\).  Solving the equation of \\(L_i\\) together with \\(x+y=c\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   x+(x+k+1-2i)=c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   x=\\frac{c-(k+1)+2i}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(c\\le k+1\\) and \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), the right\u2011hand side is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(c-1\\); consequently the point  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(x,\\;c-x\\bigr)\\in D_c\n   \\]\n\n   lies on \\(L_i\\).  In particular, for each diagonal \\(D_c\\) the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) provide **exactly one** point of \\(D_c\\) each, and the \\(k\\) points are distinct because the corresponding \\(x\\)-coordinates are distinct.  \n\n   Hence the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers **all** points of the omitted diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5.4 \u2013 verification of the covering.*  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to a kept diagonal (covered by that diagonal) or to an omitted diagonal (covered by one of the lines \\(L_i\\)).  Therefore the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n   *Step\u202f5.5 \u2013 distinctness of the lines.*  \n   The \\(n-k\\) kept diagonals are pairwise distinct.  The \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) have distinct intercepts \\(k+1-2i\\); consequently they are distinct from each other and from any diagonal (all diagonals have slope \\(-1\\)).  Thus we indeed have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   - Configurations exist for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\).  \n   - No configuration exists for \\(k=2\\) (Lemma\u202f4).  \n   - For every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) the construction of \u00a75 provides a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n   \\]"

