            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) except \\(k=2\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   For an integer \\(c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1)\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(D_c\\) is a line of slope \\(-1\\) (hence *non‑sunny*) and contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **\\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).  \n   All are non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **\\(k=1\\).**  \n   Keep the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) and replace \\(D_2\\) (which consists only of \\((1,1)\\)) by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y=2x-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\{S\\}\\cup\\{D_c\\mid 3\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line (its slope is different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\)).  \n   Then for every diagonal \\(D_c\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap D_c\\) consists of at most one point.  \n   *Proof.*  Write \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq-1\\).  \n   Solving together with \\(x+y=c\\) gives \\((1+m)x=c-b\\); because \\(1+m\\neq0\\) there is at most one solution. ∎\n\n   The three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n   \\]\n\n   lie on the two smallest diagonals \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\).  \n   Any line that contains two of them is either vertical \\((x=1)\\), horizontal \\((y=1)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)); all three are *non‑sunny*.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).\n\n   Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines existed.  \n   The \\(n-2\\) remaining lines are non‑sunny, hence each of them is a diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \n   Since there are only \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, at most \\(n-2\\) of the diagonals can be kept; therefore at least two of the diagonals among \\(\\{D_2,D_3\\}\\) must be omitted.  \n   Omitted diagonals mean that their points have to be covered by sunny lines.  \n   But the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) belong to the omitted diagonals, and we have just seen that two sunny lines cannot cover all three of them.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist. ∎\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Step 5.1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Keep the \\(n-k\\) largest diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together contain all points whose sum is at least \\(k+2\\).\n\n   *Step 5.2 – the omitted diagonals.*  \n   The omitted diagonals are  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their total set of points is  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 5.3 – the \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=-(i+1)x+(i+1)(k+2).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-(i+1)\\); it is different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\), therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The lines are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n   *Intersection with a diagonal.*  \n   Let \\(c\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le c\\le k+1\\).  \n   Solving (1) together with \\(x+y=c\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   x+ \\bigl(-(i+1)x+(i+1)(k+2)\\bigr)&=c\\\\\n   \\Longrightarrow\\; -i\\,x&=c-(i+1)(k+2)\\\\[2mm]\n   \\Longrightarrow\\; x&=\\frac{(i+1)(k+2)-c}{i}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(2\\le c\\le k+1\\), the numerator in (2) is a positive integer not exceeding \\(i(c-1)\\); a short calculation shows  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le c-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((x,c-x)\\) given by (2) lies in the set \\(D_c\\).  \n   Moreover, for a fixed \\(c\\) the values of \\(x\\) obtained from (2) are distinct when \\(i\\) varies, because the right‑hand side of (2) is strictly decreasing in \\(i\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) intersect the diagonal \\(D_c\\) in exactly the \\(c-1\\) points of \\(D_c\\) (the extra intersections correspond to values of \\(i\\) for which \\(x\\) would be \\(0\\) or \\(c\\); those do not occur because of the bounds above).\n\n   *Step 5.4 – covering of \\(S_k\\).*  \n   For each \\(c\\) the diagonal \\(D_c\\) is completely covered by the intersections with the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\); therefore every point of \\(S_k\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n   *Step 5.5 – distinctness of all \\(n\\) lines.*  \n   The \\(n-k\\) kept diagonals have slope \\(-1\\); the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) have slopes \\(-2,-3,\\dots ,-(k+1)\\).  \n   Hence all \\(n\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n   We have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  This works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   *   \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are possible (constructions in §2 and §3).  \n   *   \\(k=2\\) is impossible (Lemma 4).  \n   *   For every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) the construction of §5 provides a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
